 of attempting to break or exploit a system to identify vulnerabilities—has emerged as a vital methodology for evaluating code agent safety. Traditional red-teaming approaches, which rely on human experts manually probing systems for weaknesses, are becoming increasingly impractical as code agents grow in complexity and scale. This has motivated the development of automated red-teaming agents that can systematically explore the behavior space of code agents and identify potential vulnerabilities.\n\nThis paper reviews the current state of research in code agents and red-teaming. We begin by surveying the foundational architecture that enables modern code agents. We then discuss recent advances in automated red-teaming for code agents, including novel frameworks for generating adversarial prompts and evaluating agent safety. Finally, we review benchmark datasets and evaluation methodologies designed to assess the risks associated with code agent behavior.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\n### 2.1 Architectural Foundations of Code Agents\n\nThe development of sophisticated code agents has been built upon foundational advances in deep learning, particularly in natural language processing and sequence modeling. The Transformer architecture, introduced in \"Attention Is All You Need\" [1], revolutionized the field by demonstrating that attention mechanisms alone could achieve state-of-the-art results in sequence transduction tasks without relying on recurrent or convolutional structures. This architecture forms the backbone of modern large language models, including those powering today's most advanced code agents. The self-attention mechanism allows these models to capture long-range dependencies in code and natural language, enabling them to understand complex programming constructs and generate syntactically and semantically correct code.\n\n### 2.2 Automated Red-Teaming for Code Agents\n\nRecent work has focused on developing automated systems for red-teaming code agents. In 