n\n\nThe field of code agents represents a transformative application of AI in software development, offering unprecedented capabilities for automating coding tasks. However, the same power that makes code agents valuable also makes them potentially dangerous, necessitating rigorous safety evaluation through red-teaming methodologies.\n\nThis paper has surveyed recent advances in this rapidly evolving area, highlighting three key contributions to the field. The architectural foundations established by the Transformer architecture continue to enable increasingly sophisticated code agent systems. Automated red-teaming approaches, exemplified by RedCodeAgent, are becoming essential for systematically evaluating code agent safety at scale. And benchmark frameworks like RedCode provide the standardized infrastructure needed to measure and compare safety improvements over time.\n\nDespite these advances, several challenges remain. The diversity of code agent architectures and deployment scenarios means that no single evaluation framework can capture all safety concerns. The evolving nature of adversarial attacks requires red-teaming methodologies to continuously adapt. And the trade-off between agent capability and safety necessitates careful design choices in agent development.\n\nFuture work should focus on developing more comprehensive and adaptive evaluation frameworks, improving the interpretability of red-teaming results, and establishing industry standards for code agent safety. As code agents become increasingly integral to software development workflows, the research community must prioritize the development of robust safety evaluation methodologies to ensure these powerful tools are deployed responsibly.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, Jakob Uszkoreit, Llion Jones, Aidan N. Gomez, Lukasz Kaiser, and Illia Polosukhin. \"Attention Is All You Need.\" *arXiv preprint arXiv:1706.03762*, 2017.\n\n[2] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yan